{{/*
  The payment-status poll's answer, and the page it belongs to.

  A QR hand-over has no browser redirect back: the shopper pays in an app, on a
  phone that is very often not the device showing this page. Without this the page
  would sit unchanged while the order quietly went paid. The block is what htmx
  swaps every few seconds; the page around it is what somebody opening the URL
  directly gets, so the route is never a bare fragment in an address bar.

  It reports and grants nothing: only an authenticated gateway notification can
  make an order paid, and this only ever reads the result.
*/}}
{{define "content"}}
<h1>Payment status</h1>
<div id="payment-status">
{{template "checkout_status_block" .}}
</div>
{{end}}

{{define "checkout_status_block"}}
{{if not .HaveOrder}}
  <p class="muted">We have nothing to confirm for this basket yet.</p>
{{else if .Paid}}
  <p><strong>Payment received</strong> for order {{.Order.Reference}}.
  <a href="/cart/checkout/success">See your order</a>.</p>
{{else if .Failed}}
  <p><strong>That payment did not go through.</strong>
  <a href="/cart">Your basket is still here</a> if you would like to try again.</p>
{{else}}
  {{/*
    Still pending, so keep asking. The trigger lives on the element the response
    replaces, so the new copy carries it and the polling continues — and stops by
    itself as soon as one of the branches above renders instead.

    5 seconds, not 1: a shopper is unlocking a phone and typing a PIN, and the
    limiter this route sits behind allows 30 a minute.
  */}}
  <p hx-get="/cart/checkout/status" hx-trigger="every 5s" hx-swap="outerHTML" class="muted">
    Waiting for your payment…
  </p>
{{end}}
{{end}}
